insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 1, 'The engine returns two condition numbers with every least squares fit. Which one decides whether it refuses the design?', '["`conditionNumber`, the raw 2-norm figure of the design as given, because it carries the column of ones", "Either of the two, so a design is refused as soon as one or the other passes 1.00e+8", "`scaledConditionNumber`, measured after every column of the design is scaled to unit length", "The raw figure after each feature is centred on its training mean, which the engine does before measuring"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal reads the scaled number alone, and the basis says so: "2-norm condition number of the design with unit-length columns; refused above maxCondition 100000000". The raw number mixes units and is printed so a reader can see how much of the apparent trouble is units. The engine does not centre the features: centring is arithmetic the course did outside the fit, and it moved the attribute design from 7608.495043 to 8.661304.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 2, 'On the training rows of the teaching split, adding the depth to the three logs moves the raw condition number from 5595.200723 to 751074.623153 and the scaled one from 193.359232 to 225.474144. How does the course read that change?', '["As a units story: the depth column is large, and it comes nowhere near being a copy of the logs", "As a collinearity story, because the raw number grew far more than the scaled number did when the depth joined", "As a near refusal, because the raw figure is what maxCondition is compared with and it rose most", "As evidence that depth carries no sonic signal, since its scaled number moved less than the logs'' own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A large raw number beside a modest scaled one is a units story; a large scaled number is a collinearity story, and only that one is acted on. The depth in thousands of feet beside a density in g/cm3 inflates the raw figure whatever the data, and the scaled figure barely moved. maxCondition is compared with the scaled number, 225.474144 here, far below 1.00e+8. A condition number says nothing about signal: the training R-squared rose from 0.683457 to 0.698882.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 3, 'The logs and the four well-level attributes read a scaled condition number of 7608.495043 on the training rows. Every feature is then centred on its training mean. What changes?', '["The scaled number falls to 8.661304 and the training R-squared falls with it, as the attribute signal sat in the offsets from zero", "The raw number falls to 1251.765912 while the scaled number holds at 7608.495043, since scaling had already removed the units", "Neither number moves, as the engine centres every feature itself", "The scaled number falls to 8.661304 while the training R-squared stays at 0.861914"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Centring brings the raw number to 1251.765912 and the scaled number to 8.661304, and it leaves the fit unchanged: the R-squared of the training fit moves by 0. Centring changes where the columns sit relative to the intercept, and the fitted plane stays where it was. Unit-length scaling removes units and leaves near-collinearity with the column of ones in place, which is why the scaled number was large before centring.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 4, 'Over the training rows easting has a mean of 414.173333 km and a population SD of 0.772284 km. Why does that make the attribute design close to collinear?', '["Its unit is the kilometre while the logs carry their own units, and the scaled number keeps that mismatch", "Beside an intercept, a column far from zero that varies little is nearly a multiple of the column of ones", "Easting names the well, and any feature that names a well raises the scaled condition number by itself", "Its population SD is below 1 km, so unit-length scaling divides the column by a small number and inflates it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Scaling each column to unit length removes the units, so what the scaled number measures is how close one column comes to a combination of the others. Easting at 414.173333 km with an SD of 0.772284 km is almost a constant, and a constant is a multiple of the column of ones; northing behaves the same way. Naming a well is the leakage finding, a different property. Unit-length scaling divides each column by its own length, which for easting is large.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 5, 'A fifth column exactly twice NPHI is added to the three logs. How did the course read its scaled condition number, 6.22e+16, as a returned field?', '["It read the figure printed inside the refusal at the default, which is the field a lesson reasons with", "It centred NPHI and its copy first, which let the fit through at the default limit of 1.00e+8", "It raised maxCondition to 1.00e+300, so the call was fitted and the field came back", "It fitted ridge at lambda 0 on the same columns, since ridge returns the number that ols refuses on"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A refusal returns `error` and `field` and no numeric field, so the course raised the limit to 1.00e+300 to have the number returned. The message''s figure, 62175765717049960, is part of a sentence for a person and is quoted only as the engine''s words. An exact copy stays an exact copy after centring. Ridge at lambda 0 refuses the same design in the same words, since the refusal rule belongs to both.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 6, 'Why does the default refusal limit sit at 1.00e+8?', '["At kappa = 1.00e+8, kappa^2 x machine epsilon is 2.220446, so the worst-case error bound reaches the coefficient itself", "It is the largest scaled condition number among the NIST problems the engine fits, rounded up to a power of ten as an empirical ceiling", "Above it the smallest coefficient LRE of any fit falls below 7.66, the float-design limit that Filip reaches", "Every scaled condition number above it belongs to an exact copy of a column, as the twice-NPHI design shows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A least squares solution can lose up to about kappa^2 x machine epsilon of relative accuracy in the worst case. With epsilon 2.220446049250313e-16, kappa = 1.00e+8 gives 2.220446: an error bound as large as the coefficient, so no digit of some coefficient can be guaranteed. The limit is a stated worst-case bound. Filip''s 5.21e+9 is above it without any exact copy, and the NIST problems fitted at the default read at most 43275.043587.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 7, 'A reader passes maxCondition 43275.043587, the figure the course prints for Longley''s scaled condition number, expecting a fit exactly at the limit. What can the printed figure tell them?', '["A fit, because a value exactly at the limit is fitted and this is Longley''s value exactly", "That the call is fitted once the engine rounds its own number to six decimals before comparing", "A warning beside the fit, which is what the engine returns for a design sitting exactly at its limit instead of a refusal", "Nothing decisive: the print is a rounding to six decimals, and the rule compares the full number with the limit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A value exactly at the limit is fitted, and that is true of the full number. 43275.043587 is six decimals of a number with more digits, which can lie on either side of it; printed alike is not equal. The pair of calls that pins the rule is 43000, refused, and 44000, fitted. Least squares carries no warning: the engine declined the fit-and-warn alternative, so a design is fitted or refused.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 8, 'At the default limit the engine refuses Filip, the degree ten polynomial of 82 rows and 11 coefficients. What does the refusal say about the design?', '["Its 82 rows are too few for 11 coefficients, so the residual degrees of freedom fall below the stated minimum", "Its scaled condition number, 5.21e+9 in quotable form, is above 1.00e+8, so some coefficients could carry no reliable digits", "Its certified values were computed in multiple precision, which a float64 least squares fit cannot compare with", "It holds an exact copy of a column, the same finding the twice-NPHI design gave at 6.22e+16"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The message is the ill-conditioning refusal: the scaled condition number, printed in full as 5206821213.915052, is above maxCondition 100000000. kappa^2 x epsilon is then 6.02e+3, far above 1. Filip has 82 rows for 11 coefficients, well clear of the rule that needs more rows than coefficients. Its columns are powers of one variable: nearly collinear, with no column an exact copy of another.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 9, 'Raised knowingly to maxCondition 1.00e+10, Filip is fitted with a smallest coefficient LRE of 7.66 against a float-design limit of 7.66. What does the course conclude?', '["The missing digits are an input limit, and the two figures are claimed to agree only at the two decimals printed", "The engine''s LRE equals the float-design limit exactly, so the fit loses no digit the data offered", "The engine lost Filip''s digits to its own arithmetic, since every other problem reached at least 13", "Filip''s R-squared, at 10.37, shows the coefficients are good to ten digits once the limit is raised"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The float-design limit is how many certified digits the float64 inputs allow: the exact solution of the design as rounded to float64. No method reaches the certified values from float64 data to more digits than that, so Filip''s shortfall belongs to the input. The two figures print alike, and printed alike is not equal. The R-squared LRE of 10.37 is a different figure from the worst coefficient''s 7.66: quote the LRE of the figure you use.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 10, 'What does an LRE of 14 say about an engine coefficient compared with its certified value?', '["The engine''s figure differs from the certified one by 14 units in the last printed decimal place", "Fourteen of the problem''s coefficients agree with their certified values to the full float precision", "The first fourteen significant digits agree: minus log10 of the relative error is 14", "The two agree to fourteen decimal places after the point, whatever the size of the coefficient"]'::jsonb, 2, 'LRE = -log10(|estimate - certified| / |certified|), a count of agreeing significant digits built from the relative error, so it does not depend on the coefficient''s size or on decimal places. The course quotes the smallest LRE over a problem''s coefficients, the worst one. When the two are identical in float the relative error is zero, and the LRE is capped at 16.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 11, 'Wampler5 reads a smallest coefficient LRE of 16.00 and an R-squared LRE of 13.73. What does the pair teach?', '["The cap of 16 shows the engine''s R-squared is wrong in its fourteenth digit on Wampler5, where the certified value has more digits", "Wampler5 is the worst of the NIST problems, as its R-squared carries the fewest digits in the whole table", "Once every coefficient reaches the cap, the R-squared LRE is copied down from the smallest of them", "A coefficient''s digits and R-squared''s digits are different figures, so quote the LRE of the figure you use"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The coefficient LRE and the R-squared LRE of one problem need not be alike: Wampler5 matches every certified coefficient at the cap, and its R-squared reaches 13.73. Filip shows the other order, 7.66 on a coefficient and 10.37 on R-squared. Filip''s 10.37 is the fewest R-squared digits in the table.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 12, 'Norris reads a smallest coefficient LRE of 14.06 against a float-design limit of 14.07. How does the course read that row?', '["The engine exceeds the float-design limit, a sign that the certified value itself carries a rounding", "The two print differently, and the engine''s figure is the lower of the two", "They are equal, as every NIST problem but Filip meets its limit to the precision shown", "It is a defect in the engine''s arithmetic, since Norris is a straight line with a scaled number of 2.800505"]'::jsonb, 1, '14.06 is below 14.07, so the engine''s worst coefficient sits just under the ceiling the float64 inputs set. Pontius prints 13.51 against 13.51 and Wampler2 13.20 against 13.2: those print alike, which claims agreement at the precision shown and nothing finer. Norris prints differently, so no equality is claimed there. A shortfall of that size beside the ceiling marks no arithmetic defect.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 13, 'Leaving Filip aside, which NIST problem has the smallest coefficient LRE in the course''s table, and what does it read?', '["Wampler2, at 13.20, which is why the course says every problem but Filip agrees to at least 13 digits", "Pontius, at 13.51, as the only problem below fourteen digits apart from Filip", "Longley, at 14.62, the hardest design of the set by its scaled condition number of 43275.043587", "NoInt2, at 15.34, because a fit with no intercept loses the most digits"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Read the column: Norris 14.06, Pontius 13.51, NoInt1 14.72, NoInt2 15.34, Longley 14.62, Wampler1 16.00, Wampler2 13.20, Wampler3 to Wampler5 16.00. The smallest is Wampler2''s 13.20, below Pontius''s 13.51. Longley has the largest scaled condition number of the problems fitted at the default while its LRE is 14.62, and NoInt2 reads 15.34, above every problem except the four Wamplers at the cap.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 14, 'A column of zeros, CALI, is passed to `ols` among the features. What does the engine return?', '["The ill-conditioning refusal, with a scaled condition number printed in full above the default maxCondition of 1.00e+8", "A fit with the CALI coefficient set to 0, since a zero column contributes nothing to the prediction on any row of the table", "A refusal naming `y`, because a column with zero variance leaves R-squared undefined", "A refusal naming `X.CALI`, in its own words: X.CALI is zero in every row, so its coefficient is not identifiable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine names the column and its reason: a coefficient on a column of zeros could take any value without changing a single fitted value. The engine sets no coefficient to 0 on its own. The zero variance refusal named `y` is about a target that never varies, a different call.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-conditioning-and-nist', 15, 'Which statement about the refusal limit is true of the engine as the course states it?', '["It belongs to `ols` alone, since ridge''s penalty always makes the design well enough conditioned to fit", "It refuses at the limit and above, so a scaled number equal to maxCondition is refused", "It belongs to both `ols` and `ridge`, and a scaled condition number exactly at maxCondition is fitted", "It can be switched off by passing maxCondition 0, which the engine reads as no limit at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary table gives `ols` and `ridge` the same rule, a scaled condition number above maxCondition is refused, and the same boundary, "exactly at the limit is fitted"; ridge measures its penalised system, and at lambda 0 it refuses the exact copy of NPHI as ols does. A limit below one is refused by name: maxCondition must be a finite number, 1 or more. Raising the limit is how a fit is let through knowingly, as Filip was at 1.00e+10.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 1, 'Logistic regression on PHIC alone is passed the 106 Ekene rows with RT at or above 10 ohm.m, with l2 at 0. What does the engine return?', '["A fit after 100 updates with converged false, its PHIC coefficient still growing when the default maxIter ran out", "A refusal naming `y` before any Newton step, as PHIC at 0.16 puts every row strictly on its own class side", "Very large coefficients from an iteration that ran on, with a warning attached that says the labels are separated", "The quasi-complete refusal, since rows with a PHIC of exactly 0.16 sit on the plane carrying both labels"]'::jsonb, 1, 'On those rows the RT half of the pay rule is always met, so PAY is 1 precisely when PHIC is at least 0.16, and the engine refuses in its own words: "y is completely separated by a linear combination of the features (every row lies strictly on its own class side of a hyperplane), so the maximum likelihood coefficients are infinite: add an L2 penalty (l2 > 0) or remove the separating feature". It decides before iterating, so there is no iterate and no warning. The rule makes a PHIC of exactly 0.16 pay, so it decides every tie.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 2, 'Why are the maximum likelihood coefficients infinite when the classes are completely separated?', '["The information matrix is singular at beta = 0, so no Newton step can be solved from the start", "The separated rows start with probabilities of exactly 0 and 1, which makes the first log likelihood infinite", "With l2 at 0 the penalty term is zero, and the engine divides by it when it sizes each Newton step", "The log likelihood keeps rising as the coefficients grow, so no finite point is its largest value"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Keep the boundary where it is and steepen the curve: every probability moves closer to its own label and the log likelihood rises, with no largest value. At beta = 0 every row''s probability is one half, never 0 or 1. The penalty (l2 / 2) x sum b_j^2 is subtracted from the objective and is never a divisor. The engine settles separation before any Newton system is solved.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 3, 'The engine''s own six rows are X [[1],[2],[3],[3],[4],[5]] with y [0,0,0,1,1,1]. Which kind of separation does it find?', '["Quasi-complete: a threshold at x = 3 puts every row on its own side or on it, with both labels at 3", "Complete, as a threshold between 3 and 4 puts every class 1 row above it and every class 0 row below", "None, since the two rows at x = 3 share an x and differ in label, so no hyperplane can hold them apart at all", "Complete, because the labels rise with x through every row of the table and never once fall back to 0"]'::jsonb, 0, 'No threshold divides the classes strictly, because the two rows at 3 carry one label each, and one of them lies below any threshold between 3 and 4. The threshold at 3 puts every row on its own side or on the plane, with rows of both classes on it, so the engine refuses with the words "y is quasi-completely separated" at the head of its message. Complete separation would need a threshold with no row on it, and the pair at 3 rules that out.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 4, 'The two separation refusals share their remedy. Which clause of the quasi-complete message tells it apart from the complete one?', '["so the maximum likelihood coefficients are infinite, a claim the complete message never makes", "add an L2 penalty (l2 > 0) or remove the separating feature, which the complete message leaves out", "every row lies on or on its own class side of a hyperplane, some exactly on it", "every row lies strictly on its own class side of a hyperplane, the clause missing from the complete message"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both messages name `y`, both say the maximum likelihood coefficients are infinite, and both offer the same two remedies. The difference is where the rows lie: strictly on their own class side for complete separation, on or on their own side with some exactly on the plane for quasi-complete. They are different findings, so quote the one the engine returns.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 5, 'How does the engine build the matrix S that its separation check works on?', '["It weights each row by its probability at beta = 0, then centres every column on its mean over the rows passed", "It standardises each column with the population SD of the rows passed, the way ridge scales before a fit", "It multiplies each row by +1 when y = 1 and -1 when y = 0, then divides each column by its largest absolute value", "Stacking the pay rows over the non-pay rows, it scales each of the two blocks to unit length"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis names it the "column-scaled, sign-flipped design S". After the sign flip, a coefficient vector that scores every pay row positive and every non-pay row negative scores every row of S positive, so separation becomes S beta > 0. The column scaling is by the largest absolute value in each column; no mean, SD or probability enters.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 6, 'In the course''s statement of Gordan''s theorem, which programme does the engine solve, and what does an infeasible answer mean?', '["S''w = 0, sum w = 1, w >= 0; infeasible means some beta has S beta > 0, so the labels are completely separated", "S beta > 0 for beta itself; a feasible answer means complete separation, and no w is ever formed by the engine", "S''w = 0, w >= 1; infeasible means the labels are completely separated and the second programme is then skipped", "S''w = 0, sum w = 1, w >= 0; a feasible answer is the one that proves the labels completely separated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gordan''s theorem says exactly one of two things holds: some beta has S beta > 0, which is complete separation, or some w >= 0 with sum 1 has S''w = 0. The engine solves the second as a linear programme, and if it is infeasible the first must hold. A feasible Gordan programme rules complete separation out. The programme with w >= 1 is Stiemke''s, the second one.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 7, 'When does the engine solve its second programme, S''w = 0 with w >= 1, and what does each answer mean?', '["Always, straight after Gordan''s; feasible means quasi-complete, and infeasible means no separation of any kind", "Only when Gordan''s programme is infeasible, to tell complete separation apart from the quasi-complete case", "Just for penalised fits with l2 above zero, which need to know the kind of separation they are fitting through", "Only when Gordan''s programme is feasible; feasible means no separation, and infeasible means quasi-complete"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis states the order: "COMPLETE when S''w = 0, sum w = 1, w >= 0 is infeasible (Gordan); otherwise QUASI-COMPLETE when S''w = 0, w >= 1 is infeasible (Stiemke), else none". Stiemke''s theorem says either some w >= 1 has S''w = 0, no separation, or the separation is quasi-complete. An infeasible Gordan programme already decides complete. The check runs at every l2: the l2 = 1 fit on the high-RT rows reports complete.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 8, 'The course calls the engine''s separation decision exact. Exact up to what?', '["The Newton tolerance of 1.00e-10, since the check watches the first few steps for coefficients that keep growing", "The linear programme''s feasibility tolerance: infeasible means an artificial sum above 1e-7 after phase one", "The singularity rule of `solveSPD`, p x machine epsilon, applied to the information matrix at beta = 0 before any step", "A stated cutoff on coefficient size, above which the engine calls the labels separated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each pair of alternatives is a theorem, so once feasibility is known the kind of separation follows with no judgement left over; the one tolerance is the programme''s own, stated in the basis. The check runs before any Newton step, so neither the stopping rule nor `solveSPD` enters. Watching coefficients grow is the common alternative the engine declined, and it leaves someone choosing how large is large.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 9, 'The Professional tier''s pay model, RHOB, NPHI and RT with an intercept on the 210 training rows, reported `separation.type` none. What certificate did the engine print?', '["\"weights w >= 1 with S''w = 0 (Stiemke)\", so no hyperplane in those features puts pay and non-pay on their own sides", "None at all, because a fit that finds no separation has nothing to prove and reports the type alone", "An infeasible Gordan programme, the proof that no hyperplane divides pay rows from the rest", "PHIC at 0.16, the threshold of the stated pay rule that the model had to learn from the logs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'By Stiemke''s theorem, weights w >= 1 with S''w = 0 exist only when there is no separation of any kind, so those weights are the certificate for none, and the fit went on to its finite answer. An infeasible Gordan programme means complete separation, the opposite finding. The pay model never saw PHIC: the rule reads it, the model reads RHOB, NPHI and RT.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 10, 'With l2 = 1 on the 106 high-RT rows, logistic regression on PHIC is fitted. What does the result report?', '["A PHIC coefficient of 0.817700 with `separation.type` none, as the penalty took the separation away", "The PHIC coefficient 6.814258 after 6 iterations, converged true, with a warning about the separation", "Another refusal naming `y`, since a penalty cannot make a separated fit finite and the refusal message says as much", "A PHIC coefficient of 0.817700 log odds per v/v after 6 iterations, converged true, `separation.type` complete"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With the penalty the objective is bounded and the fit is finite: PHIC 0.817700, intercept 1.881712, 6 iterations, converged true. The separation is still there, and the result says so with `separation.type` complete. 6.814258 is the coefficient at l2 = 0.1. A converged fit carries no warning, and the refusal''s own remedy is "add an L2 penalty (l2 > 0)".'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 11, 'On the same rows the PHIC coefficient is 6.814258 at l2 = 0.1 and 0.817700 at l2 = 1. What does the pair show?', '["The coefficient settles near 6.814258 as l2 falls further, which is the maximum likelihood value the refusal at l2 = 0 withheld", "A weaker penalty gives a larger coefficient, and as l2 goes to zero there is no finite limit for it to settle at", "l2 = 0.1 fits the data better, as its coefficient is nearer the size a porosity effect should have", "They are two estimates of one quantity, and their spread measures the uncertainty in the PHIC effect"]'::jsonb, 1, 'On separated rows the likelihood alone would take the coefficient to infinity, and the value you read is where the penalty stops it: the coefficient is set by the penalty. So a penalised coefficient on separated data is quoted with its l2, and coefficients fitted at different l2 values are never compared as estimates of one number. There is no finite maximum likelihood value to settle at.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 12, 'What does logistic regression subtract from the log likelihood when l2 is above zero?', '["l2 x sum b_j^2 over every coefficient, the intercept included, so that every term shrinks alike", "l2 x sum |b_j| over the non-intercept coefficients, a penalty that sets some coefficients exactly to zero", "(l2 / 2) x sum b_j^2 over the non-intercept coefficients, which makes the objective bounded", "Half of l2 times the sum of b_j^2 over every coefficient divided by the number of rows, so larger tables are penalised less"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The penalty (l2 / 2) x sum b_j^2 runs over the non-intercept coefficients, as ridge leaves its intercept unpenalised. The log likelihood can never rise above zero while the penalty grows without limit, so a best coefficient exists. The penalty is on squares, and it is not divided by the number of rows.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 13, 'Why does the engine decide separation before its first Newton step?', '["A separated fit with no penalty has no finite answer to print, and deciding first leaves no size cutoff to choose", "Newton steps on separated rows use all 30 halvings at the first iteration, which the check spares", "The linear programmes need beta = 0 as their start, and one Newton step would move away from it", "The stopping rule has to know the kind of separation before it can set its tol in coefficient units"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s stated reason for its choice: a separated maximum likelihood fit has no finite answer to print. The common alternative is to iterate and watch the coefficients grow, which leaves someone choosing how large is large and prints a number set by the stopping point. The programmes work on S, built from the design and the labels, and no coefficient enters them. The stopping rule''s tol is set by the caller.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 14, 'The separation refusal offers two remedies. On the high-RT rows, which one serves a model meant to predict pay from the logs?', '["Adding l2 = 1, since the penalised PHIC coefficient of 0.817700 is then a finite estimate of the porosity effect", "Lowering the RT cutoff until the separation turns quasi-complete, a kind the engine fits with no penalty", "Removing PHIC: it is an input of the rule that made the label, so a model using it is handed part of the answer", "Raising maxIter to its limit, so Newton''s method runs on until the PHIC coefficient settles"]'::jsonb, 2, 'PHIC is the label in disguise on those rows, and the Professional tier''s pay model, which reads RHOB, NPHI and RT and never PHIC, had no separation to fit through. A penalised coefficient on separated rows is set by the penalty and says as much about l2 as about the rock. Quasi-complete separation is refused at l2 = 0 as well, and a separated fit never reaches a Newton step for maxIter to extend.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-separation', 15, 'A report says the group split ''separated'' the test wells from the training wells. How does the course''s vocabulary treat that word?', '["It is right, since a group split puts every row of a well on one side of the split, which is complete separation by definition", "Right for a group split and wrong for a random-row split, where rows of one well fall on both sides", "Acceptable whenever the `sharedGroups` list is empty, which is the engine''s own check for it", "Separation names the logistic property of labels on their own sides of a hyperplane; a split is called a split"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course legislates the word: separation is the logistic property, a hyperplane putting the classes on their own sides (complete) or on or beside it (quasi-complete). A split is a split, whichever kind it is. `sharedGroups` lists the wells a random-row split puts on both sides; it describes a split, and the separation check reads labels and features.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 1, 'Where does the engine start the Newton steps of a logistic fit, and what does every row''s probability read there?', '["At the least squares coefficients of the 0 and 1 labels on the features, so the first step starts near the answer", "From the coefficients the last call on the same features returned, reused as a warm start to save iterations", "At beta = 0, where every row''s probability is one half", "With the intercept at 0 and every other coefficient at 1, so that each feature starts out carrying weight"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis states it: "Newton-Raphson (IRLS) from beta = 0". With every coefficient zero the log odds are zero for every row, so every probability is 1 / (1 + exp(0)), one half. From there the pay fit''s first step is 1.024456 at its largest component, and the log likelihood rises to -46.305377.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 2, 'What does one Newton step of a logistic fit solve?', '["The information matrix against the score, a symmetric positive definite system solved with `solveSPD`", "The unweighted normal equations of the 0 and 1 labels on the features, the same system least squares solves once", "A line search along the score alone, stepping forward until the log likelihood stops rising", "The two separation programmes again, once per iteration, to confirm that no hyperplane has formed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the current coefficients the score is the slope of the log likelihood in each coefficient and the information matrix its curvature; the step solves one against the other with `solveSPD`. It is a weighted least squares solve with weights p(1 - p), which is why it is also called IRLS. Separation is decided once, before the first step.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 3, 'On the Ekene pay fit, at which iteration does the engine stop, and why there?', '["At iteration 7, the first whose log likelihood prints -24.507027, since the likelihood stops changing from there on", "At iteration 9, whose largest step component of 9.80e-9 is small enough to count as converged under the tol", "At iteration 100, the default maxIter, since the engine always takes every update it is allowed to take", "At iteration 10, whose step of 1.14e-13 is the first at or below 1.00e-10"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rule stops the fit when the largest component of the full Newton step is at most tol, 1.00e-10 by default. 9.80e-9 is above that, so iteration 9 does not stop it, and 1.14e-13 at iteration 10 is the first step at or below it. A flat printed likelihood is no stopping rule, and maxIter is a ceiling: the fit stops as soon as the rule is met.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 4, 'The pay fit''s log likelihood prints -24.507027 at iterations 7, 8, 9 and 10. What does the course take from that?', '["The fit had converged by iteration 7, and the last three steps did no more than confirm a solution already reached", "Printed alike is not converged: the coefficients still moved by 0.097613 and 0.000448 at steps 7 and 8", "The stopping rule reads the log likelihood at six decimals and stops once the print holds still", "Steps 8 to 10 were halved away to almost nothing, leaving the likelihood nowhere to move"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The likelihood''s changes no longer show at six decimals from iteration 7, while the step, in coefficient units, is still 0.097613 at iteration 7 and 0.000448 at iteration 8. The engine judges convergence on the step, never on a printed likelihood. The halvings column reads 0 on every row of the trace, so every step was taken whole.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 5, 'Which statement of the logistic stopping rule is exact?', '["Stop once every coefficient''s relative change falls strictly below tol, whatever the units of its feature", "Stop when the log likelihood rises by less than tol x (1 + |l|) over two iterations in a row", "Stop when the largest component of the step actually taken, after any halving, falls strictly below tol", "Stop when the largest absolute component of the full Newton step is at most tol, in coefficient units"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "converged when the largest absolute component of the full Newton step is at most tol 1e-10 (in coefficient units: set tol to the scale of the coefficients when features are in very small or very large units); at most maxIter 100 updates". The rule is absolute, inclusive and read on the full step. The allowance 1e-12 x (1 + |l|) belongs to the halving rule, a different test.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 6, 'A logistic fit''s full Newton step comes out with a largest component exactly equal to tol. What does the engine do?', '["It stops, because the rule is inclusive: at most tol", "It takes one more step, since convergence needs a step strictly below tol, as a refusal needs a number strictly above its limit", "Halving the step and testing again, since the halving rule is what settles a tie that lands exactly on tol", "It returns converged false with a warning, because a step at tol sits on the edge of the tolerance, outside it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each rule draws its own boundary. The logistic stop is inclusive, so a step exactly at tol stops the fit. The condition limit is the other way round: a number exactly at maxCondition is fitted and only one strictly above is refused. The halving rule is strict and is about a fall in the penalised log likelihood; it never reads tol.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 7, 'Which iteration of the pay fit takes the largest Newton step, and what do the steps do after it?', '["Iteration 1, at 1.024456, because Newton''s method takes its biggest step from the starting point at beta = 0", "Iteration 3, at 5.791631, the step the maxIter 3 warning reports", "Iteration 4, at 7.440994; the steps then shrink and collapse over the last few iterations", "Iteration 5, at 5.014085, after which the steps shrink at a steady rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trace reads 1.024456, 2.796103, 5.791631, 7.440994, 5.014085, 1.434323, 0.097613, 0.000448, 9.80e-9 and 1.14e-13, so the largest is iteration 4''s. The steps grow while the coefficients travel from zero, then shrink. Near the solution Newton''s method roughly squares the error each step, so the fall is anything but steady. Iteration 3''s step is indeed the one the maxIter 3 warning reports, and it is smaller than iteration 4''s.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 8, 'The pay fit is run with maxIter 3. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming `maxIter`, since three updates are too few for the fit to reach tol on these rows", "The full result with `converged` false and a warning, its RT coefficient the third iterate, 0.196989", "The converged RT coefficient 0.241141, since the engine carries on past maxIter until the step reaches tol", "An RT coefficient of 0.196989 with converged true, because the answer after three updates is the fit asked for"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A fit that stops early is no refusal: the inputs were fine and the engine was allowed three updates. It returns every field a fitted model carries and adds `converged` false and the warning, in its own words: "did not converge in 3 updates: the last full Newton step had a largest component of 5.791630774699914, above tol 1e-10". maxIter is obeyed; 0.241141 is the RT coefficient at convergence, after 10 iterations.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 9, 'The pay fit stopped at maxIter 3 warns, in the engine''s words, "did not converge in 3 updates: the last full Newton step had a largest component of 5.791630774699914, above tol 1e-10". Which figure does a lesson reason with?', '["The trace field for iteration 3, 5.791631 at six decimals", "The message''s own figure, since a warning carries every digit the engine computed and the trace rounds them", "The tol of 1e-10 inside the message, as the only figure a warning is built to report", "Iteration 4''s step, 7.440994, which the stopped fit would have taken next had maxIter allowed it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A message is a sentence for a person, and its figure prints as the shortest decimal that reads back to the number. A lesson quotes the numeric field at six decimals, and the message only verbatim as the engine''s own words. The trace field is the same full step the converged fit took at its third iteration: same data, same start, same steps.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 10, 'After 3 updates the pay fit reads a log likelihood of -25.899408, against -24.507027 at convergence. How does the course read the iterate''s coefficients and standard errors?', '["As a regularised estimate, since stopping Newton''s method early shrinks the coefficients the way an L2 penalty does", "As usable coefficients with wider standard errors, which the engine inflates to account for the steps not taken", "As a point on the way that estimates nothing, reported only as the iterate after 3 updates with the warning beside it", "As the converged answer in all but name, because the log likelihood had risen at every one of the three steps"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The iterate''s last full step, 5.791631, is far above tol, and the steps after it were never taken. Its standard errors are computed at the iterate too and carry no more meaning than its coefficients. A rising likelihood is what every Newton step on this fit did, all the way to iteration 10. Never report a coefficient from a fit with `converged` false as if it were fitted.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 11, 'The engine''s own case has rock compressibility in 1/Pa, largest value 8.52e-10, over 40 rows. What happens at tol 0.1 and at the default tol?', '["At 0.1 it stops too early to be trusted, while at 1.00e-10 it converges in 6 iterations, as the default suits any feature", "Refused at both, as a feature of order 8.52e-10 is too ill-conditioned", "It converges at both, as the rule measures each step relative to its coefficient", "At 0.1 it converges in 6 iterations; at 1.00e-10 it runs all 100 updates and warns"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The compressibility coefficient is near 6.83e+9 log odds per 1/Pa. At the default tol the last full step is still above 1.00e-10 after 100 updates, so the fit returns converged false with a warning; the rule compares the step with tol in absolute terms, whatever the coefficient''s size. With tol 0.1 it converges in 6 iterations. Nothing is refused: the case is fitted both times.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 12, 'The compressibility coefficient is near 6.83e+9. What is a tol of 0.1 on it in relative terms, and what does that show?', '["A relative 0.1, since the engine divides every step by its coefficient before it compares the step with tol", "A relative 1.46e-11: a tol that looks loose in absolute terms is tight beside a coefficient that large", "A relative 6.83e+9, since the tol is multiplied up by the size of the coefficient it guards", "A relative 1.00e-10, the default, which is why tol 0.1 and the default tol return the same fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Relative to a coefficient near 6.83e+9, a step of 0.1 is 1.46e-11. The engine divides nothing: the step is compared with tol in coefficient units. The two tols did not return the same fit: 0.1 converged in 6 iterations, and the default ran to 100 updates with converged false.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 13, 'A fit on a feature in very small units runs to maxIter at the default tol. Which remedies do the basis and the course name?', '["Set tol to the scale of the coefficients, or rescale the feature, to 1/GPa for instance", "Add an L2 penalty (l2 > 0), which the engine names as its remedy for any fit that does not converge", "Raise maxCondition knowingly, since a feature in small units is refused as ill-conditioned until it is", "Remove the feature, because the warning shows that it carries no signal the fit can use"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis says: set tol to the scale of the coefficients when features are in very small or very large units. The other remedy rescales the feature, which changes the units the coefficient and its steps are measured in. The L2 penalty is the separation refusal''s remedy, maxCondition belongs to least squares and ridge, and a warning says the fit stopped early, which is no finding about signal.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 14, 'When does the engine halve a Newton step?', '["Whenever the full step is above tol, until the halved step reaches tol", "When the step raises the log likelihood by less than 1e-12 x (1 + |l|), up to the 100 updates maxIter allows", "Each time the step lowers the plain log likelihood at all, by even the smallest amount, up to 30 times", "When the step lowers the penalised log likelihood by more than 1e-12 x (1 + |l|), up to 30 times"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis: "Newton-Raphson (IRLS) from beta = 0; a step is halved while it lowers the penalised log likelihood by more than 1e-12 x (1 + |log likelihood|), up to 30 halvings". The comparison is strict, so a fall of exactly the allowance is kept. With l2 at zero the penalised and plain log likelihood are the same. The pay fit halved 0 times.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-convergence', 15, 'Why does the engine judge convergence on the full Newton step, before any halving?', '["The full step is cheaper, since each halved step needs a second solve of the Newton system to be formed", "Halving happens only on penalised fits, so reading the full step keeps one rule for every value of l2", "A halved step shrinks each time it is cut, so a fit stuck far from its answer could otherwise halve its way below tol", "The full step and the halved step are always equal on any fit that goes on to converge, so either would do"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s stated reason: the full step cannot be faked by halving. If the rule read the halved step, enough halvings would make any step tiny and could report converged true far from the answer. A halving reuses the solved step and tries it at half the length. Halving guards the penalised log likelihood at every l2, and at l2 = 0 that is the plain log likelihood.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 1, 'How does permutation importance measure a feature on a fitted model?', '["It drops the feature, refits the model on the rest, and reads how far the test error rises without it", "Multiplying the coefficient by the feature''s population SD gives its size in standard units, which it reports as the importance", "It shuffles the target across the rows and reads how far each coefficient moves when the model is refitted", "It shuffles that feature across the scored rows, predicts again without refitting, and reads the loss of score"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shuffling one feature''s values across the rows breaks its link to the target and keeps its distribution. The model is not refitted: permutation importance asks what this model does without this feature''s information. A coefficient carries the target''s unit over the feature''s, and its size says nothing about importance until the feature''s spread is known, which is why importance is measured directly.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 2, 'For RMSE the engine''s drop is permuted RMSE less baseline RMSE. What is the drop for AUC, and why?', '["Permuted less baseline, the same arithmetic as for RMSE, so a feature that matters gives a negative AUC drop", "Baseline less permuted, so a drop is positive when the feature matters, whatever the metric", "The raw permuted AUC, since higher AUC is already better", "One minus the permuted AUC, the share of pay and non-pay pairs the shuffled model now puts in the wrong order"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis for RMSE reads "permuted rmse - baseline rmse (positive when the feature matters)", and for AUC, where higher is better, the drop is baseline less permuted. One sign for every metric is the engine''s stated choice; the common alternative is the raw permuted score, which leaves the sign to the reader.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 3, 'On the teaching setting, OLS on GR, RHOB, NPHI and CALI scored by test RMSE with 5 repeats and seed 5, how do the four features rank?', '["GR 4.747694, NPHI 3.786311, RHOB 1.754752, CALI -0.031931 us/ft, largest mean drop first", "NPHI first, as NPHI''s coefficient on the three logs, 138.783590 us/ft per v/v, is the largest in size", "GR, RHOB, NPHI, CALI, the column order the features were passed in, which is the order the engine ranks by", "NPHI, GR, RHOB, CALI, since the engine ranks the features by their SD over the five repeats"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ranking is by mean drop, largest first: GR, NPHI, RHOB, CALI. A coefficient''s size depends on the feature''s unit, so NPHI''s large coefficient per whole v/v ranks nothing. Column order is the order the stream draws the shuffles in. The SDs, 0.323271 for NPHI and 0.315115 for GR, say how the repeats scatter and never set the rank.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 4, 'On the teaching setting, what baseline are the drops measured from?', '["The test RMSE of the three-log fit on the teaching split, 4.282693 us/ft, as the Associate tier printed it", "The four-feature fit''s training RMSE, since importance is measured on the rows the model was fitted on", "The unshuffled test RMSE of the four-feature OLS fit on the 90 test rows, 4.309962 us/ft", "The mean k-fold test RMSE, 5.826789 us/ft"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model is OLS on GR, RHOB, NPHI and CALI fitted on the training wells of the teaching split and scored on the 90 test rows, and its unshuffled test RMSE is 4.309962. The three-log model scores 4.282693 on the same wells, a different model. Importance is measured on the rows scored, here the test wells, and the k-fold mean belongs to ridge on the three logs.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 5, 'CALI''s five drops are all negative, with a mean of -0.031931 us/ft. What does a negative drop mean?', '["On those repeats the shuffled CALI gave a slightly lower test RMSE than the real one, and the engine prints that as it is", "The engine has pushed a positive drop below zero to mark a feature that ought to be removed from the model", "CALI works against the sonic: the more the model uses it the worse the fit, so its link to DT runs negative", "CALI''s drop is taken as baseline less permuted, the sign the engine keeps for a metric where higher is better"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A drop near zero can come out slightly negative when a shuffle happens to help, and the engine never clips it: the sign is information. For RMSE every feature''s drop is permuted less baseline, CALI''s included. CALI was drawn with no link to the sonic, so a negative drop reports luck in the shuffles on these rows, which is no relationship in the rock.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 6, 'What tells you that CALI is doing nothing useful in this model?', '["The sign of its mean alone: a negative mean proves no signal", "Its last place in the ranking, which by itself shows a feature is useless whatever the size of the drops", "A coefficient of zero on CALI, which the engine sets for any feature whose importance comes out negative", "Its mean of -0.031931 beside an SD of 0.026894, both small next to RHOB''s mean drop of 1.754752"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A drop near zero is read against its spread and against the other features. CALI''s mean and SD are both small beside RHOB''s 1.754752, the smallest positive mean drop, and that comparison is the evidence; the sign alone would not be. Some feature always ranks last. Least squares gives CALI a coefficient like any other feature, and importance changes no coefficient.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 7, 'The SD printed beside each mean drop, 0.315115 for GR, is which quantity?', '["The standard error of GR''s mean drop, the sample SD of the 5 drops over the square root of 5", "The population SD of the 5 drops, divided by n, describing how these draws scatter", "The sample SD of the 5 drops with n - 1, the divisor the data quality course''s z-score uses", "The population SD of GR itself over the 90 test rows, which sets how far any shuffle can move it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SD is the population SD of the 5 drops. GR''s drops are 4.955540, 4.682248, 4.227650, 4.704944 and 5.168088 us/ft, mean 4.747694, and 0.315115 describes their scatter; it is no standard error of the mean. The standard deviation always names its divisor in this course, and the data quality course''s z-score is the sample SD case.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 8, 'At the same seed, why can a run with a different nRepeats read different drops for RHOB?', '["The seed is multiplied by nRepeats before the stream starts, so each repeat count runs its own stream", "Each feature is seeded by the seed plus its column index, and nRepeats shifts that index along", "One stream serves the call, features in column order with repeats inner, so GR''s repeats use draws that RHOB''s would get", "The drops are averaged with weights set by nRepeats, so the same draws give a different mean"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis: "mulberry32(seed), one stream for the call; features in column order, repeats inner; Fisher-Yates from the end over the row order; row i takes the value from row perm[i]". GR''s shuffles come first, so the number of GR repeats decides where in the stream RHOB''s begin. The mean drop is a plain mean of the drops.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 9, 'At seed 6 the mean drops read GR 4.954327, RHOB 1.752107, NPHI 3.651470 and CALI -0.022098. What follows?', '["The seed 5 figures were wrong, since a correct importance cannot depend on the seed that drew its shuffles", "GR''s rise from 4.747694 shows the model leans harder on GR at seed 6 than it did at seed 5, with the same rows and coefficients", "The ranking changed, as NPHI fell below RHOB once the shuffles were drawn from the seed 6 stream", "Every mean moved and the ranking held, GR, NPHI, RHOB, CALI, so an importance is quoted with its seed and repeats"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The model and rows are the same at both seeds; only the shuffles differ, so the movement is the draw. At seed 6 NPHI''s 3.651470 is still above RHOB''s 1.752107, and the ranking is GR, NPHI, RHOB, CALI at both seeds. Without its seed and repeats an importance cannot be reproduced, and a reader cannot tell the model from the draw.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 10, 'On the pay model scored by AUC on its test wells, baseline 0.997475, the mean drops read RT 0.398232, RHOB 0.019823 and NPHI 0.001263. How do they compare with the sonic model''s drops?', '["They are never compared: an AUC drop and an RMSE drop in us/ft are in different units", "RT''s 0.398232 sits far below GR''s 4.747694, so RT matters less to its model than GR does to its own", "They rank on one scale with the sonic drops, because both kinds are positive when the feature matters", "Every AUC drop is below 1, so each pay feature matters less than a sonic log whose drop is above 1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One sign for every metric makes both kinds positive when the feature matters, and it leaves them in their own units: AUC is a probability and the RMSE drop is in us/ft. Within the pay model the ranking is RT, RHOB, NPHI. Quote each importance with its metric, model, rows, seed and repeats.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 11, 'A reader takes GR''s mean drop of 4.747694 as proof that gamma ray controls sonic slowness in the rock. What does the course''s vocabulary say?', '["The reading is right, since a shuffle breaks the feature''s link to the target just as nature would", "It holds once the SD over the repeats, 0.315115, is small beside the mean drop it goes with", "Importance is the loss of score for this fitted model, on these rows, with this seed and these repeats", "The reading holds on the training wells alone, because the drops were measured on the rows the model was fitted on"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course legislates the word: importance is the loss of score when the feature is shuffled, for this fitted model, on these rows, with this seed and these repeats. It describes a feature the model leans on, whether or not the rock does, and says nothing about cause. The drops were measured on the 90 test rows of the test wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 12, 'How does `learningCurve` choose its test wells and add its training wells on the teaching setting?', '["It draws a fresh groupSplit at every point, so the test wells change each time a training well is added", "One groupSplit at fraction 0.3 and seed 5 fixes EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8, and the other six join in the split''s shuffled order", "Training wells join in sorted name order, EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2 and on, so the curve needs no seed", "The test wells are the last three of the shuffled order, and training wells join from the front of that order"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The split''s shuffled order is EKENE-8, EKENE-4, EKENE-5, EKENE-2, EKENE-1, EKENE-3, EKENE-10, EKENE-9, EKENE-7: the first three are the test wells and stay fixed for every point, and the training wells join as EKENE-2, EKENE-1, EKENE-3, EKENE-10, EKENE-9, EKENE-7. So the point at m wells is always the point at m - 1 wells with one more well added.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 13, 'Why does the engine count a learning curve''s size in wells?', '["Every well holds 30 rows here, so counting in wells is a shorter way to write the row counts and carries no other reason", "The engine cannot divide a well''s rows, so any size given in rows would be rounded to whole wells", "Counting in wells is the scikit-learn convention, and the engine matches scikit-learn wherever it can so that results compare", "Rows of one well are not independent: 30 more rows from a well already in the fit carry its offset again"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each Ekene well sits above or below the fitted plane as a block, and the well means of the residuals span 12.562737 us/ft. Rows from a new well bring a new offset, so each step on the curve is one more well of the kind the model will be asked about. The common alternative counts rows, and the engine''s stated reason for wells is that rows of one well are not independent.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 14, 'Along the Ekene learning curve, at which added wells does the test RMSE rise?', '["At EKENE-10, from 4.385966 to 4.761367, and at EKENE-7, from 4.205658 to 4.282693", "Only at EKENE-10; after that well the test RMSE falls at every added well through to the end", "Nowhere: it falls at every added well, from 6.766479 at one well down to 4.282693 at six", "At EKENE-1 and EKENE-3, the second and third wells, where the training RMSE rises as well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test RMSE reads 6.766479, 4.756657, 4.385966, 4.761367, 4.205658 and 4.282693 as wells are added, so it rises twice: at the fourth well, EKENE-10, and at the sixth, EKENE-7. It falls at EKENE-1 and EKENE-3. The order of the wells is the shuffle''s, and a well with a large offset added early or late moves the curve.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-importance-and-learning-curves', 15, 'At one training well the curve reads 2.749195 on the training rows and 6.766479 on the test wells; at six wells, 5.758010 and 4.282693. Which reading is right?', '["The model fits its training wells better at six, as the training RMSE then rests on more rows", "Test beating training at six wells shows the split leaked, since honest test scores always sit above training", "The training score is the fit to wells the model has seen, and the test score the error on wells it has not", "The lowest test RMSE of the curve is its last point, 4.282693, which makes six the best number of wells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A model fitted to one well is fitted to that well''s rows, its own offset included, so it scores well on them and badly on new wells. As wells join, the fit spans several offsets and its training RMSE climbs to 5.758010, with a dip from 4.351546 to 4.346620 at the third well. Test below training at six wells is one draw of wells that happen to sit close to the plane. The lowest test RMSE on the curve is 4.205658, at five wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 1, 'The forty rows from 130 to 169, the last twenty of EKENE-5 and the first twenty of EKENE-6, are passed to least squares with DT as the target. What does the engine return?', '["A fit on the twenty EKENE-5 rows alone, since the engine drops a row whose target is null before it fits the plane", "A refusal naming `X[20][1]`, the engine''s message for a null it finds in the feature matrix of a least squares fit", "A refusal naming `y[20]`, the twenty-first row passed, because EKENE-6''s first sample is dataset row 150 and its DT is null", "A fit with the twenty null targets filled by the training mean of DT, and a warning that lists the filled rows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Row 150, counted from 0, is EKENE-6''s first sample, and it is the twenty-first of the forty rows passed, so the refusal names `y[20]` in the engine''s own words: "y[20] must be a finite number". The engine neither fills nor drops a missing value; that decision is the caller''s. `X[20][1]` is the refusal for a null in the features, a different call.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 2, 'Five candidates were scored on the same k 3, seed 5 folds of the nine sonic wells. Which does the course choose for EKENE-6, and on what figure?', '["Ridge on GR, RHOB and NPHI at lambda 10, the lowest mean test RMSE on those folds, 5.826789 us/ft", "Least squares on the three logs, 5.888677 us/ft, since a penalty only adds bias", "Ridge on GR, RHOB, NPHI and CALI at lambda 10, at 5.851480 us/ft, since a fourth log can only add information to the fit", "The attribute set at lambda 100, 6.773053 us/ft, as it carries each well''s offset"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The lowest mean over the three folds is the three logs at lambda 10, 5.826789 us/ft. The logs at lambda 0 read 5.888677 and the logs with CALI at lambda 10 read 5.851480, both higher on the same wells. The attributes at their best, lambda 100, read 6.773053: they name the wells they were fitted on, and a new well''s offset is absent from its training rows.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 3, 'On the same folds, adding CALI to the three logs reads 5.908607 against 5.888677 at lambda 0, and 5.851480 against 5.826789 at lambda 10. What does the course read from the comparison?', '["Both pairs differ in the second decimal only, so the course treats CALI as neutral and keeps it for its extra log coverage", "Adding CALI lowers the mean at both lambdas, which is why the course carries the caliper forward into the write-back", "Sharing one set of folds makes the two sets impossible to compare, since every CALI fit reuses the logs-only fits'' rows", "Scored on wells the model has not seen, the caliper raises the mean at both lambdas and earns no place in the prediction"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both differences have the same sign: the mean rises with CALI, 5.908607 above 5.888677 and 5.851480 above 5.826789. CALI was drawn with no link to the sonic, and on wells the model has not seen it does not help. Scoring every candidate on the same folds is what makes a difference between two means a difference on the same wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 4, 'What does the mean test RMSE of 5.826789 us/ft estimate?', '["The error EKENE-6''s predicted sonic will carry, since that well is the one the chosen model is fitted to predict", "The error expected for a new well like the nine, since every fold held out wells drawn from those nine", "An upper limit on the error of any row predicted, the flagged rows above the GR range included, by the chosen fit", "The training RMSE of ridge at lambda 10 on all 270 sonic rows, the fit applied to EKENE-6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mean is taken over folds that each held out three of the nine sonic wells, so it describes a new well of the same kind. On this synthetic field EKENE-6 reads 13.176539 against its withheld sonic, far above 5.826789, because it differs from the nine. The write-back claims no error for rows outside the range, and a training RMSE is scored on rows the fit has seen.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 5, 'Leaving one well out, k 9, the Professional tier read 5.495600 for the logs at lambda 0 and 5.501614 at lambda 10. How should the choice of lambda 10 be quoted?', '["As proven for every well, since the k 3 folds and the nine folds both test each of the nine wells once in the comparison", "As overturned, since leaving one well out is the more thorough scheme and so it replaces the k 3 result in the write-back", "As the lowest mean on the k 3, seed 5 folds, stated with those folds and that seed, and never as best for every well", "As a tie, since 5.495600 and 5.501614 print alike once rounded to two decimals, so either lambda may be quoted"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At k 3 lambda 10 is lower, 5.826789 against 5.888677; at k 9 lambda 0 is lower, 5.495600 against 5.501614. Another k can reverse the order, so the choice is quoted with the folds and seed it was made on. The two k 9 means print alike at two decimals and differently at six, and two figures are never taken as equal from how they print.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 6, 'Step 2 fits the model that predicts EKENE-6. Which fit is it?', '["Ridge at lambda 10 on all 270 sonic rows, GR''s coefficient 0.292658 us/ft per gAPI in original units", "Least squares on the 180 training rows of the teaching split, GR''s coefficient 0.288005 us/ft per gAPI", "The fold 0 fit of the k 3 comparison, the model that scored the three wells EKENE-10, EKENE-2 and EKENE-8", "Ridge at lambda 10 on the six training wells of the teaching split, so that its three test wells stay unseen"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Once the features and lambda are chosen by the folds, the prediction is fitted on every well that has the log: ridge at lambda 10 on all 270 rows of the nine wells, with coefficients 2.521897, 0.292658, 21.134015 and 137.387028 for the intercept, GR, RHOB and NPHI. 0.288005 is least squares on the teaching split''s 180 rows, and a fold''s model trained on six wells only.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 7, 'Min-max scaling is fitted on the nine sonic wells and applied to EKENE-6. What does it report?', '["Four rows on each of GR, RHOB and NPHI map above 1, since the hot shale raised every log EKENE-6 carries", "None of the 30 rows, since the scaler clips a new row to [0, 1] before the model ever sees it", "All 30 rows on GR, since the hot shale adds 30 gAPI to every EKENE-6 sample and so lifts each one out of range", "On GR, 4 of the 30 rows map above 1, the highest to 1.219324; RHOB and NPHI stay inside the range on every row"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The scaler maps EKENE-6''s GR to between 0.320959 and 1.219324, with 4 rows above 1; RHOB reaches 0.925040 and NPHI 0.600000, both inside. The hot shale is on GR alone. Min-max does not clip, which is why a row outside the training range shows as a value above 1, and 26 of the shifted rows still sit inside the nine-well range.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 8, 'One EKENE-6 row maps to a scaled GR of 1.219324. What does that value say?', '["A z-score: the row''s GR sits 1.219324 population SDs above the training centre of the nine sonic wells", "That row''s GR is above the nine-well maximum of 120.870000 gAPI, so on it the model extrapolates", "The scaler has failed on that row, since a min-max value is bounded by 0 and 1 for every row it is applied to", "The row''s GR is above the nine-well mean by the hot shale''s 30 gAPI, which the scaler shows as a ratio"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Min-max maps the training minimum, 21.200000 gAPI, to 0 and the training maximum, 120.870000, to 1, and it does not clip a new row. A value above 1 is the scaler saying the row lies outside the range the model was fitted on. A z-score divides by an SD about a centre, which is the standard scaler''s map; min-max divides by the training range.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 9, 'The course checks EKENE-6''s prediction against its withheld sonic. What makes that check possible?', '["A sonic run later in EKENE-6 over the same interval, which the course compares with the predicted values row by row", "The k-fold folds, which tested EKENE-6 once when it was dealt into a fold with two of the nine sonic wells", "Leaving EKENE-6 out of the fit makes its null DT samples a test set, which the engine scores against the prediction", "The field is synthetic: its builder kept the sonic drawn for EKENE-6 apart from the rows, and a real well gives no such check"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EKENE-6''s DT is null on every row; the sonic drawn for it was kept as `withheld` and the course reads it once, saying so. A real field has no such record: if the sonic had been run no one would predict it. EKENE-6 was never in any fold, since the folds are over the nine wells that carry a DT, and a null target cannot score anything.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 10, 'Predicted less withheld averages 12.708542 us/ft over EKENE-6''s 30 rows. Which stated causes does the course give for a bias that size?', '["The 4 rows above the GR range, whose extrapolated values alone lift the mean of the whole well to 12.708542", "Ridge shrinkage at lambda 10, which pulls every prediction toward the nine-well mean DT and away from what the rock itself gives", "The hot shale, 30 gAPI times GR''s 0.292658, adding 8.779741, and the well''s own offset of -5.138713 that no prediction can know", "The k-fold estimate of 5.826789 counted twice, once in the fit and once in the write-back line of the channel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two stated causes are the hot shale, 0.292658 x 30 = 8.779741 us/ft on every row, and EKENE-6''s planted offset less the nine-well mean, -5.138713. 8.779741 less -5.138713 is 13.918453, within 1.209912 of the mean error. The bias sits on every row, inside the range as well as above it, so the 4 flagged rows cannot carry it alone.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 11, 'The 4 flagged rows read an RMSE of 14.922136 against the withheld sonic, and the 26 rows inside the GR range read 12.887017. What does the second figure show about the range check?', '["It sees only rows that leave the range: the 26 rows inside it carry the same hot shale shift and still pass", "The check did its job, since the rows it passed are close to the k-fold estimate of 5.826789 for a well like the nine", "Twenty-six clean rows and four bad ones, the pattern a range check exists to find and one this well shows", "It proves the 26 rows are fine to use, as a row inside the training range has the expected error of the folds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The flagged rows are worse, 14.922136, and the 26 unflagged rows read 12.887017, far above 5.826789. The hot shale shifted all 30 rows by 30 gAPI and moved only 4 of them past the nine-well maximum. A range check tests where a row sits against the training range, so a shift that keeps a row inside that range passes it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 12, 'A well is shifted in a way that keeps its logs inside the training range. How does the course say such a shift is found?', '["By a min-max scaler fitted on more wells, which narrows the training range until the shifted rows fall outside it", "By the k-fold mean test RMSE, which rises for any well that differs from the ones the folds were drawn from", "Its predictions are refused by the engine once they sit far enough from the training mean of the target", "By comparing its logs with its neighbours'', the data quality course''s work, and by the geology; the scaler never finds it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A range check sees only the rows that leave the range, and EKENE-6''s 26 shifted rows stayed inside it. The course names two ways to find such a shift: compare the well''s logs with its neighbours'', which the data quality course teaches, and the geology. More wells can only widen a training range. The k-fold mean never scores the new well, and the engine has no refusal for a prediction''s size.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 13, 'Where does the course''s rule write EKENE-6''s predicted sonic?', '["Into the DT channel itself, with each predicted row marked as predicted, so the well has one sonic", "Into a new channel, DT_PRED, beside the measured DT, which stays null", "Over the measured DT of EKENE-6, since that channel holds only nulls and so nothing measured is lost", "Into a project file kept apart from the well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule writes a new channel, DT_PRED, beside the measured DT, and the measured DT stays null. A prediction written into the DT channel would erase the fact that EKENE-6 has no sonic, and a later fit on every well with a DT would read the model''s output as data. The prediction belongs with the well, labelled as what it is.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 14, 'Which expected error does the write-back carry, and with what basis?', '["5.826789 us/ft, the mean test RMSE of k-fold by wells, k 3 and seed 5, for a new well like the nine", "13.176539 us/ft, EKENE-6''s RMSE against the withheld sonic, since it is that well''s own measured error", "5.495600 us/ft, the leave one well out mean for the logs at lambda 0, the most thorough scheme the course ran", "12.887017 us/ft, the RMSE of the rows inside the GR range, since those are the rows the flags leave unmarked"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule''s expected error line is "k-fold by wells, k 3, seed 5: mean test RMSE 5.826789 us/ft". The withheld comparisons exist only because this field is synthetic, and a real field offers none. 5.495600 belongs to least squares under a different k, while the model written back is ridge at lambda 10 chosen on the k 3 folds.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-missing-log-prediction', 15, 'What does the write-back say about the rows outside the training range, and about the other rows?', '["The 4 flagged rows are dropped from DT_PRED, since a row the model extrapolates on carries no predicted value", "The 4 flagged rows carry an error of 14.922136 us/ft and the other 26 an error of 5.826789 us/ft, each row stamped with its own", "The 4 rows with GR above 120.870000 gAPI are flagged row by row, no error is claimed for them, and nothing is claimed as a measurement", "The 4 flagged rows are clipped to the nine-well maximum of GR and predicted again, so every row is in range"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rule reads "4 of 30, GR above the nine-well maximum 120.870000 gAPI; flagged row by row" and, under what is not claimed, "no error for rows outside the range, and no measurement". The flagged rows keep their predictions with the flag beside them. 14.922136 comes from the withheld sonic, which no real well has, and the engine does not clip a new row.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 1, 'The engine''s standard scaler divides by n. What reason does the engine state for that choice?', '["It matches the data quality course''s z-score, which divides by the same n", "Ridge lambda then equals scikit-learn''s alpha on the same standardised features", "Dividing by n gives the unbiased estimate of each feature''s spread", "The n - 1 divisor would make the scale depend on the seed of the split"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convention table gives the reason as ridge lambda equalling scikit-learn alpha on the same features. The data quality course''s z-score uses the sample SD, divisor n - 1, which is the alternative this engine declined. On 180 rows the two scales differ by the factor 1.002789, so the divisor is named every time a standard deviation is quoted.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 2, 'A colleague runs a group split in another library with seed 5 and fraction 0.3 on the nine sonic wells and holds out different wells from EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8. How does the course compare the two splits?', '["By the seed, since two tools given seed 5 and the same fraction draw the same shuffle of the same wells", "The other library is in error, as a seeded group split has one right answer for a stated seed and fraction", "By the count of test rows, 90 in each, which is all a group split promises whatever the tool that draws it", "By the list of wells each one holds out, since no library reproduces the engine''s mulberry32 draws"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every draw here is mulberry32 with a stated seed, one canonical generator across the platform, and the convention table says no library reproduces its draws, so splits are compared by well list. A seed fixes the draw inside one generator only. Two tools holding out 90 rows each can still hold out different wells, and a score on one set is a score on those wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 3, 'With k = 4 on the nine sonic wells, how do the engine''s folds come out, and what does scikit-learn''s GroupKFold balance instead?', '["3, 2, 2 and 2 wells, 90, 60, 60 and 60 rows; GroupKFold balances rows and takes no seed", "Four folds balanced by their rows, the aim both tools share, each of them taking a seed to deal", "Folds of 3, 3, 3 and 0 wells, as round robin fills each fold in turn", "The same folds as GroupKFold, since both deal whole wells in sorted order"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Round robin deals the well at shuffled position q to fold q mod k, so fold sizes differ by at most one well: 3, 2, 2, 2 wells, or 90, 60, 60, 60 rows. The engine balances the count of wells and is seeded; GroupKFold balances rows and takes no seed. Filling one fold before the next would leave a fold empty.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 4, 'Why does the engine''s ROC curve start at a point whose threshold is null?', '["The first point has no rows called positive, so the engine leaves the threshold for the caller to supply", "A null threshold marks the curve as unscored until the AUC is taken by the trapezoid rule over its points", "JSON has no infinity, the value scikit-learn prints for that first threshold", "Null stands for a threshold of 0, where every row is called positive"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The curve starts at (0, 0) with threshold null, and the convention table gives the reason: scikit-learn prints infinity there, and JSON has no infinity. At (0, 0) no row is called positive, which is a threshold above every score. A threshold below every score would call every row positive, the curve''s last point, (1, 1).'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 5, 'The engine writes F1 as 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN). How does that compare with the harmonic mean of precision and recall?', '["Always a little lower, as the counted form weights FN and FP unequally", "It is equal wherever both precision and recall are defined, and it is defined in more cases", "A different score that agrees with the harmonic mean only on a binary report with two supported labels", "Undefined in more cases, since it divides by zero whenever TP is 0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convention table states it: the counted form is equal to the harmonic mean wherever both are defined, and defined in more cases. On the pay label TP 24, FP 4 and FN 0 give the 0.923077 printed. A label with no true positive still has a nonzero denominator when it has a false positive or a false negative, so the counted form gives 0 there.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 6, 'A row with y = 1 and a probability of exactly 0 is charged 34.538776 by this engine''s log loss. What would scikit-learn 1.9 charge, and why?', '["36.043653, since it clips at the float64 machine epsilon, 2.22e-16, where this engine clips at 1.00e-15", "34.538776 as well, since both tools clip a probability of 0 at the same stated eps before taking the log", "An infinite charge, since scikit-learn 1.9 takes no eps and so leaves the zero probability as it is given", "0.693147, the charge of a probability of one half, which is what scikit-learn puts in place of a zero"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This engine clips at eps = 1.00e-15 and charges -ln(1.00e-15) = 34.538776. scikit-learn 1.9 takes no eps and clips at the float type''s machine epsilon, 2.22e-16 for float64, so it charges -ln(2.22e-16) = 36.043653. Taking no eps is different from not clipping. 0.693147 is ln 2, the loss when every probability is one half.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 7, 'Where does the log loss clip draw its boundary?', '["Every probability below 1.00e-10 is clipped, the logistic tol", "A probability equal to eps is clipped along with everything below it, so p = 1.00e-15 clips 1 row", "Only a probability of exactly 0 or 1 is clipped, since any other value has a finite logarithm to take", "A probability equal to eps is kept: p = 1.00e-15 clips 0 rows, and p = 1.00e-16 clips 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clip is to [eps, 1 - eps] and the boundary table reads "a probability equal to eps is kept": p = 1.00e-15 clips 0 rows and p = 1.00e-16 clips 1. The logistic tol is a different rule with a different constant. A value of 1.00e-16 is finite and nonzero and is still clipped, because it lies below eps.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 8, 'A feature reads [2, 2, 2.0000000001] on the training rows, and another reads [2, 2, 2]. What does the standard scaler do with each?', '["It refuses both: a spread of 4.71e-11 counts as zero variance", "Both are fitted, the second with a scale of 0", "It fits the first, with a scale of 4.71e-11, and refuses the second as constant", "It fits the second and refuses the first as noise in the tenth decimal"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A feature is refused as constant only when every training value is identical, and the boundary table shows it: [2, 2, 2.0000000001] is fitted with a scale of 4.71e-11 and [2, 2, 2] is refused. The rule is about identical values, and a small spread is still a spread. Scaling by 0 would divide by zero, which is why the engine refuses.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 9, 'Leaving one well out asks `groupKFold` for k equal to the count of wells. What happens at k = 9 and at k = 10 on the sonic wells?', '["k 9 is refused since a fold of one well leaves too few rows, and k 10 is refused with it", "k 9 is fitted as leave one well out, and k 10 is refused: k must be a whole number from 2 to 9", "k 9 and k 10 are both fitted, since k 10 simply leaves one of the ten folds with no test well", "k 9 is fitted, and k 10 is quietly lowered to 9, the number of distinct wells"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is 2 <= k <= number of groups, and k equal to the number of groups is allowed: k 9 on nine wells is leave one well out. k 10 is refused in the engine''s own words, "k must be a whole number from 2 to 9 (the number of distinct groups)". A refusal changes no input on the caller''s behalf, and an empty fold would test nothing.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 10, 'Least squares with an intercept on one feature is passed 2 rows, then 3. What does the engine do?', '["It refuses 2 rows for 2 coefficients and fits 3 rows, with 1 residual degree of freedom", "Both are fitted, since 2 points always define a line and the fit simply has no residual to report", "Both are refused, since one feature needs at least 4 rows before the engine will estimate s", "It fits 2 rows exactly and warns that the standard errors are undefined, then fits 3 rows as usual"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Least squares needs more rows than coefficients, and the boundary is n = p refused: 2 rows for 2 coefficients, the intercept counted, are refused, and 3 rows are fitted with n - p = 1. With n = p the line passes through every row and leaves no degree of freedom to estimate s. The engine refuses that case rather than warn.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 11, 'A Newton step lowers the penalised log likelihood by exactly 1e-12 x (1 + |l|). Is the step halved?', '["Yes: the halving rule is inclusive like the stopping rule, so a fall equal to the allowance is halved", "Yes, and the halving then repeats until the fall is below the allowance, up to 30 times", "The step is refused, since a Newton step that lowers the objective ends the fit with a warning", "No: the halving rule is strict, and only a fall of more than that allowance is halved"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The boundary table gives the halving rule as a fall of more than 1e-12 x (1 + |l|), strict, so a fall exactly at the allowance is kept whole. The stopping rule is the inclusive one: a full step at most tol stops the fit. Each rule draws its own boundary. A step that lowers the objective is halved, never refused, and the pay fit halved 0 times.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 12, 'A team asks the engine for a random forest facies model that returns a prediction interval with each value. What does the course say?', '["The engine builds the forest from its seeded shuffles and returns an interval from the spread of its repeats", "Only the interval is missing; the engine''s trees handle facies", "Neither is built: the engine fits linear models only and gives no prediction interval", "The logistic model serves, and its probability gives the interval"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine fits least squares, ridge and binary logistic regression only: no tree, forest, boosting, neural network, neighbour rule or clustering, and clustering and facies are the electrofacies course''s. It gives no prediction interval and samples no inputs. The seeded shuffles serve the splits and permutation importance only, and a logistic probability is the probability of PAY = 1 for one row.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 13, 'Which of these does the engine do?', '["Fill a missing log value with the training mean before a fit runs", "Draw seeded shuffles, for its splits and for permutation importance", "Search lambda over the folds and return the model with the lowest mean", "Hold out the last rows of each well in a time-ordered split for a rate series"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The only random draws are the seeded shuffles of the splits and of permutation importance. A missing value is refused by name, and filling or dropping it is the caller''s decision. There is no search over lambda or features: a course or an app runs the folds and prints the scores it compared. There is no time-ordered split; forecasting is its own course''s.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 14, 'A model note for the written-back sonic model, ridge at lambda 10 on the three logs, quotes the importance ranking GR, NPHI, RHOB, CALI. What must it say beside the ranking?', '["That it was measured on OLS with CALI, on the teaching split''s test wells, seed 5 and 5 repeats: another model", "Nothing more, since a ranking of the logs describes the rock and so holds for any model fitted to them", "Only the seed, 5, since the repeats and the model do not change a ranking once the seed is fixed", "That the ranking was measured on the ridge model at lambda 10 on all 270 rows, the one written back"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Importance here is the loss of score for this fitted model, on these rows, with this seed and these repeats. The ranking was measured on OLS on GR, RHOB, NPHI and CALI, scored on the 90 test rows at seed 5 with 5 repeats, and the model written back is ridge at lambda 10 on 270 rows. The drops depend on the seed and on nRepeats, since one stream serves the whole call.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-engine-honestly', 15, 'Another tool reports a test R-squared of 0.816151 on the teaching split''s test wells, where this engine prints 0.815322 for the same predictions. What explains the difference?', '["Different predictions, since two tools fitting the same least squares plane on the same rows disagree in the third decimal", "Rounding, since 0.815322 and 0.816151 are one figure printed at two precisions by the two different tools", "The divisor of the standard deviation, which the other tool takes as n - 1 on the 90 test rows it scores", "The reference mean: the engine''s default is the test targets'' mean, and 0.816151 is taken about the training mean"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both rows print the same test RMSE, 4.282693, so the predictions agree; the R-squared differs by its reference. The engine''s default is about the mean of the test targets, 105.214444, as scikit-learn''s r2_score; about the training mean, 105.883333, passed as `referenceMean`, it reads 0.816151. R-squared involves no standard deviation, so no divisor enters it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'The logs and the four well-level attributes, with an intercept, on all 270 sonic rows read a raw condition number of 473051.317118 and a scaled one of 3694.131550. What does least squares do at the default limit?', '["It refuses the design, since the raw figure is above the limit once the column of ones is counted in the design", "It fits the design with a warning, since the raw figure is large enough to cost digits in the fitted coefficients", "It fits the design, since the scaled figure is far below 1.00e+8 and the raw figure plays no part in the rule", "Refusal comes first and the engine then retries after centring, which brings the design below the refusal limit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal compares `scaledConditionNumber`, the design with unit-length columns, with maxCondition, 1.00e+8 by default. The raw figure mixes units and decides nothing. This design is fitted at the default limit, and the engine never centres a design on the caller''s behalf; the refusal message names centring as the caller''s remedy.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Longley''s scaled condition number is 43275.043587. What does least squares return at maxCondition 43000, and at 44000?', '["A refusal at 43000 and a fit at 44000, since only a figure strictly above the limit is refused", "A fit at both, since each limit is far below the default of 1.00e+8 and the default is what the rule reads", "A refusal at both, as Longley is a higher difficulty problem and the engine refuses it below the default", "A fit at 43000 with a warning, and a clean fit at 44000, the warning marking the design''s closeness to the limit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The goldens bracket the limit: `ols-longley-below-limit` at 43000 is refused and `ols-longley-limit-44000` at 44000 is fitted. The rule reads the maxCondition passed, and the default applies only when none is given. At 44000 Longley is fitted, with a coefficient LRE of 14.62.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'At Filip''s scaled condition number, kappa^2 x machine epsilon is 6.02e+3. What does that figure say?', '["Filip''s fit carries 6.02e+3 correct digits, far more than float64 can print for any of its 11 coefficients", "It sits below the limit of 1.00e+8, so Filip is fitted at the default and its coefficients can be trusted", "It is Filip''s LRE, the count of significant digits by which the engine''s value and the certified value agree", "The worst-case error bound is far above the size of the coefficient, so some coefficients could carry no reliable digit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Filip''s 5.21e+9 squared, times 2.22e-16, gives 6.02e+3: a worst-case relative error thousands of times the coefficient. The refusal rule compares kappa with 1.00e+8, and Filip is refused at the default. The figure is a bound on relative error, so it counts no digits; raised knowingly to 1.00e+10, Filip is fitted with a smallest coefficient LRE of 7.66.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'A caller passes maxCondition 0.5 to least squares. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming `X`, the message for a design whose scaled condition number is above maxCondition", "A refusal naming `maxCondition`, in the engine''s words: maxCondition must be a finite number, 1 or more", "A fit, since every design has a scaled condition number of at least 1 and so the limit is simply never met", "A fit at the default limit of 1.00e+8, the value the engine puts in place of any limit it cannot use"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The limit itself is refused by name: "maxCondition must be a finite number, 1 or more", and the field named is `maxCondition`. A refusal never substitutes a default for a bad input. A limit below 1 would refuse every design, since a condition number is at least 1, the value NoInt1 prints as 1.000000.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Another tool fits the design with a column exactly twice NPHI and prints its coefficients with a warning. Why does this engine refuse the same design at the default?', '["The copied column makes the design singular, and a singular design is the only case a least squares fit refuses", "The engine warns as the other tool does, and the course calls the warning a refusal since no coefficient is used", "Above 1.00e+8 some coefficients could carry no reliable digits, and the engine declines to print numbers it cannot vouch for", "Two columns that share one name are refused by name before the engine computes any condition number at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The convention table sets the engine''s OLS refusal, a scaled condition number above 1.00e+8, against the alternative "fit anything and warn", and gives the reason: at 1.00e+8 the worst-case bound reaches the coefficient itself. The doubled column reads 6.22e+16. The refusal is about conditioning: the doubled column is an exact copy, and the engine''s rule refuses where the alternative warns.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'To how many significant digits does the engine reach Longley''s certified coefficients?', '["At least 14 on every coefficient: the smallest coefficient LRE is 14.62", "Exactly 7, one digit for each of the 7 coefficients that Longley''s design carries", "At most 5, since its scaled condition number is 43275.043587 and the digits lost follow from it", "All 16 on every coefficient, since the engine and the certified values agree in float"]'::jsonb, 0, 'LRE is minus log10 of the relative error, so an LRE of 14.62 on the worst coefficient means at least the first fourteen significant digits agree. A condition number bounds the loss in the worst case and does not set the digits reached. An LRE capped at 16 marks an identical float, and Longley''s certified R-squared is reached to 15.48.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'The ill-conditioning refusal and the separation refusal each name a field. Which?', '["Both name `y`, since in each case the target is what the engine cannot fit with the given design", "Both name `X`, since in each case a column of the design is the cause that the remedy removes", "The conditioning refusal names `maxCondition` and the separation refusal names `l2`, the inputs to raise", "The conditioning refusal names `X`, the design; the separation refusal names `y`, the labels"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every refusal names the input it refused and its message starts with that name: "X is too ill-conditioned for a float64 least squares fit" and "y is completely separated by a linear combination of the features". `maxCondition` is named only when the limit itself is invalid, and l2 appears in the separation message as a remedy.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Two logistic fits go wrong: one on labels a threshold splits exactly, at l2 0, and one on the pay model stopped at maxIter 3. What does each return?', '["Both return results with warnings, since logistic regression refuses only an input it cannot read at all", "The first is refused before any Newton step; the second returns its result with converged false and a warning", "Both are refused by name, since neither fit reaches a converged maximum of the log likelihood it maximises", "The first returns huge coefficients with a warning; the second is refused because it did not converge"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Separation is decided before iterating, and at l2 0 it is refused: there is no iterate and no warning. A fit that stops before it converges is a result, converged false with a warning such as "did not converge in 3 updates", and never a refusal. Watching coefficients grow is the alternative the engine declined.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Logistic regression is passed only the 94 pay samples, every label 1. What does the engine return?', '["The complete separation refusal, since every row lies on the pay side of any hyperplane", "A fit whose intercept grows without limit, stopped at maxIter 100 with a warning", "A refusal naming `y`: y must contain both classes, 0 and 1", "A fit with every probability at 1.000000 and a log loss of 0 on the rows passed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A label with one class is refused by name: "y must contain both classes, 0 and 1". Separation needs both classes on their own sides of a hyperplane. No fit is returned, so there is no probability or log loss to read. 94 of the 300 Ekene samples are pay under the stated rule.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Another tool runs Newton steps on PHIC for the high-RT rows and reports a very large coefficient with a warning. What does this engine do with the same rows at l2 0, and why?', '["It decides separation exactly by linear programme before iterating and refuses, since no finite answer exists to print", "It iterates as the other tool does and stops when the coefficient passes a size cutoff stated in its basis", "It fits a finite coefficient, since the Newton step halving keeps the coefficient from growing past its limit", "Refusal comes only after maxIter updates, once the growth in the coefficient shows the labels are separated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The convention table sets the engine''s choice, decided exactly by two linear programmes before iterating and refused at l2 = 0, against the alternative "iterate and watch the coefficients grow". The reason given: a separated maximum likelihood fit has no finite answer to print. On these rows Gordan''s programme alone decides it: infeasible, so complete, and Stiemke''s is never reached. The engine''s check involves no size cutoff, and halving only guards against a step that lowers the log likelihood.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'With l2 = 1 on the 106 high-RT rows the fit reports an intercept of 1.881712 and a PHIC coefficient of 0.817700. Which of the two does the penalty act on?', '["Both of them, each by the same (l2 / 2) x b^2 term, so the intercept shrinks toward zero as well", "The intercept only, since shrinking it toward the mean label is what makes the separated fit finite", "Neither of them, since at l2 = 1 the penalty is too weak to move a coefficient on separated rows", "PHIC''s coefficient only: the penalty sums b_j^2 over the coefficients other than the intercept"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The penalty is (l2 / 2) x sum b_j^2 on the non-intercept coefficients, and it makes the objective bounded, so the fit is finite: 6 iterations, converged true. The coefficient is then set by the penalty rather than by the data. At l2 = 0.1 the PHIC coefficient is 6.814258, so the penalty moves it a long way.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Which call finds the Ekene field''s planted pay rule as a structure, and how?', '["Logistic regression on RHOB, NPHI and RT over the 210 training rows, which reports its separation as none", "Logistic regression on PHIC alone over the rows with RT at or above 10 ohm.m, refused as completely separated", "Permutation importance on the pay model by AUC, which ranks RT first at a mean drop of 0.398232", "A group split of the ten wells at seed 5, which puts EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7 on the test side"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The planted structure table names the method that finds each item, and for the pay label it is logistic on PHIC alone, rows with RT at or above the cutoff: completely separated. The pay model on RHOB, NPHI and RT reports none, with the Stiemke certificate. An importance ranks features for one fitted model, and a split only chooses rows.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'The pay model on all 300 rows, stopped at maxIter 3, returns an NPHI coefficient of -5.491841. The same call at tol 1.00e-3 returns it unchanged. Why?', '["The fit stops by maxIter at the third Newton iterate, so the tol never comes into it", "A tol of 1.00e-3 is looser than the default, so the fit converged at the third step either way", "The engine reads tol only when maxIter is at its default of 100 updates", "Both calls converged at iteration 3, the point where the full Newton step fell below 1.00e-3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The value is the third Newton iterate, converged false: it stops by maxIter, and the same call at tol 1.00e-3 returns it unchanged. A converged fit would report converged true, and this one reports false. tol is read whatever maxIter is: the maxIter 3 warning on the Ekene pay fit compares its last step with tol 1e-10.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'With l2 = 1 on the 106 high-RT rows, the PHIC fit converges in 6 iterations. Which rule decided that it had converged?', '["A rule kept for penalised fits alone, which stops once the penalty term changes by less than l2 between steps", "A fixed count of 6 iterations that the engine sets from l2, since a penalised fit has no step to measure", "The separation check, which turns feasible once the penalty is large enough and so ends the Newton iteration", "The one every logistic fit uses: the largest component of the full Newton step at most tol, 1.00e-10 by default"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A penalised fit runs the same Newton-Raphson iteration from beta = 0 and meets the same stated rule: stop when the largest absolute component of the full step is at most tol, in coefficient units, or after maxIter updates. The separation test runs once, before any iteration, and at l2 = 1 the result still reports `separation.type` complete. The penalty makes the objective bounded; it sets no iteration count.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Which defaults govern a logistic fit when none is passed?', '["tol 1.00e-15 on the full step, the same constant as the log loss clip, and 100 updates", "tol 1.00e-9 on the relative change in log likelihood, 100 updates and 30 halvings", "tol 1.00e-10 on the full step, at most 100 updates and at most 30 halvings of a step", "tol 1.00e-10 on the log likelihood, at most 30 updates and 100 halvings of a step"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exported DEFAULTS read LOGISTIC_TOL 1.00e-10, the largest full Newton step in coefficient units, LOGISTIC_MAX_ITER 100 and STEP_HALVINGS 30. 1.00e-15 is LOG_LOSS_EPS, and 1.00e-9 is WHOLE_TOL, the test size rule. The stopping rule reads coefficient units, and the log likelihood decides only whether a step is halved.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The pay fit''s last four steps read 0.097613, 0.000448, 9.80e-9 and 1.14e-13. What does the course say produces that pattern?', '["Step halving, which cuts each step in half repeatedly as the fit approaches its answer", "Near the solution Newton''s method roughly squares the error each step, so the steps collapse", "The penalty, which grows with each iteration and damps the coefficients toward zero", "A shrinking tol, which the engine tightens by a factor at each iteration it takes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course reads the collapse over the last few iterations as Newton''s method roughly squaring the error near the solution. The pay fit halved 0 times, its l2 is 0, and tol stays at 1.00e-10 throughout. Convergence is judged on the full step before any halving, so a halving cannot shape the steps the trace prints.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The converged pay fit reports a log likelihood of -24.507027. Which deviances does it report beside it?', '["24.507027, the log likelihood with its sign changed, against 49.014054 for the intercept-only model", "267.335951 for the three features, and 49.014054 for the model that carries the intercept alone", "46.305377, from the first Newton step, since the deviance is read before the fit begins to iterate", "49.014054, which is -2 x the log likelihood, against 267.335951 for the intercept-only model"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Deviance is -2 x log likelihood: 49.014054 for the fit with RHOB, NPHI and RT, and `nullDeviance` 267.335951 for the intercept-only model. The deviance fell from 267.335951 to 49.014054 with the three features, so the fit with features has the smaller figure. -46.305377 is the log likelihood after the first step, part of the trace.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'A caller passes tol 0 to logistic regression, hoping to force an exact solution. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming `tol`: tol must be a finite number above zero", "A fit that runs all 100 updates and warns, since no step can ever reach zero", "A fit at the default tol of 1.00e-10, which the engine uses in place of zero", "An exact fit, since Newton''s method reaches a step of zero at the solution"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A tolerance of zero is refused by name: "tol must be a finite number above zero". The engine never swaps in a default for an invalid input. The rule is inclusive, at most tol, so a zero tol would ask for a full step of exactly zero.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The learning curve''s lowest test RMSE, 4.205658, comes at five training wells, and the sixth well raises it. What does the course read from that?', '["Five wells is the right training size for this model, as the test error is lowest there and any more wells only add noise", "The sixth well carries bad data, since adding a correct well to a fit can only lower the test error on the other wells", "The curve follows one stated order of wells, the shuffle''s, and a well with a large offset moves it wherever it joins", "Test RMSE and training RMSE have swapped places at the sixth well, which marks the point where the model overfits"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The test RMSE does not fall at every added well: the order of the wells is the shuffle''s, and a well with a large offset added early or late moves the curve. The curve is one order on one set of test wells, so its lowest point picks out no general best size. At six wells the training RMSE is 5.758010 and the test 4.282693, and the test was already below the training at five.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'What does a learning curve that is flat over its last points say?', '["The model has converged, as the Newton steps have fallen below tol on the last wells added to the fit", "More wells are unlikely to help: the features, the model or the well-to-well offsets now set the error", "Every added well is a copy of the ones before it, so the curve should be recut in rows", "The seed was unlucky, so the curve should be redrawn at the next seed until it falls again"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course reads a curve still falling at the last point as saying more wells would help, and a flat one as saying the features, the model, or the well-to-well offsets now set the error. The curve here is least squares, which has no Newton steps. Sizes are counted in wells because rows of one well are not independent, and choosing a seed by its result is no reading of the curve.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Permutation importance is asked for AUC on the least squares sonic model. What does the engine return?', '["The AUC drops of each feature, since any model''s predictions can be ranked by the order of their scores", "The RMSE drops instead, since the engine picks the metric that suits the model it is given", "A drop of 0 for every feature, since AUC cannot move when the model is a regression", "A refusal naming `metric`: metric auc is a classification metric and the model is ols"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The metric must suit the model, and the engine refuses the mismatch by name: "metric auc is a classification metric and the model is ols". METRICS lists auc as a classification metric. A refusal never substitutes another metric, and no drops are computed at all.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, '`learningCurve` is passed training well counts [3, 2, 4]. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming `trainGroupCounts[1]`: it must be larger than the count before it", "A curve at 2, 3 and 4 wells, since the engine sorts the counts before fitting each size", "A curve at 3, 2 and 4 wells, drawn in the order the caller passed the counts in", "A refusal naming `trainGroupCounts`, since a curve must start at one training well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The counts must rise, and the second one does not: "trainGroupCounts[1] must be larger than the count before it". The field names the first entry that breaks the rule. The engine does not reorder a caller''s input, and nothing in the rule requires a curve to start at one well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'On the pay model scored by AUC, how do RHOB and NPHI rank, and how firmly?', '["NPHI second, since its logistic coefficient, -9.876360, is the largest in size after the intercept", "RHOB and NPHI tie, as both drops print below the SD of RT, 0.015727, and so cannot be ranked", "RHOB second at a mean drop of 0.019823, SD 0.009850, and NPHI last at 0.001263, SD 0.001056", "NPHI first, since a drop of AUC is permuted less baseline and so the smallest drop matters most"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ranking is RT, RHOB, NPHI by mean drop, with RT at 0.398232. RHOB''s 0.019823 sits above its SD over the repeats, 0.009850, and NPHI''s 0.001263 is close to its own SD, 0.001056. A coefficient''s size depends on the feature''s unit, the drop for AUC is baseline less permuted, and a ranking is read from the means themselves.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Two pieces of evidence speak against CALI: its mean permutation drop of -0.031931 us/ft, and the k-fold mean rising from 5.826789 to 5.851480 when it is added. How do they differ?', '["They are the same measurement, since both score the three logs with CALI on the same three test wells at seed 5", "The drop is measured on the training rows, and the k-fold on the test wells, so only the k-fold is out of sample", "The k-fold figure is the importance of CALI in ridge, and the drop is its importance in least squares, one metric", "The drop shuffles CALI in one fitted OLS model on the teaching split''s test wells; the k-fold refits with and without it on unseen wells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Permutation importance shuffles a feature in a fitted model without refitting, here OLS on GR, RHOB, NPHI and CALI scored on the 90 test rows at seed 5 with 5 repeats. The k-fold comparison fits ridge with and without CALI on every fold, k 3 and seed 5, and scores the wells each fold held out. Both are out of sample; they answer different questions.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'EKENE-6''s first row, at 8092 ft with GR 92.370000, RHOB 2.420000 and NPHI 0.270000, is predicted at 117.793533 us/ft. Which fit produced it?', '["Least squares on the teaching split''s 180 training rows, coefficients -0.552686, 0.288005, 22.499915 and 138.783590", "The step 2 fit: ridge at lambda 10 on every well that carries a DT, all 270 sonic rows", "Ridge at lambda 10 on the six training wells of the teaching split", "Least squares on the eight wells left after EKENE-8 is held out, coefficients 8.829369, 0.320501, 17.445328 and 139.804854"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Once the folds have chosen the features and lambda, the course refits on all nine wells that carry a DT, and that refit predicts every EKENE-6 row, this one included. The two least squares sets belong to the Associate tier, one on the teaching split and one with EKENE-8 held out. A split''s six training wells would leave three sonic wells unused by the fit that is written back.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'A report says EKENE-6''s sonic was predicted by machine learning. How does the course say it should be written?', '["By its method: ridge regression at lambda 10 on GR, RHOB and NPHI, fitted on 270 rows of 9 wells", "A trained model: a term general enough to cover whichever method was used on the logs", "Machine learning is precise enough once the expected error is written beside it", "As a sonic log, since a prediction checked against a range test can stand in for a measurement of the well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In this course machine learning names a fitted statistical model by its method, least squares, ridge or logistic, so a reader can check it. The write-back''s method line reads ridge, lambda 10, features GR, RHOB, NPHI, fitted on 270 rows of 9 wells. The predicted values go into DT_PRED and are never written as a measurement.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Why must the caller keep EKENE-6''s rows out of the sonic fit?', '["The engine drops null rows only when told to, so without a flag they are fitted with a DT of zero", "Leaving them in fills EKENE-6''s DT with the training mean, which pulls every coefficient toward that mean", "EKENE-6''s hot shale makes its rows outliers, and the engine refuses any fit that includes an outlying row", "The engine neither fills nor drops a missing value: it refuses a null target by name and leaves the decision to the caller"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine does not fill a missing value: a null in X or y is refused by name, and filling or dropping it is the caller''s decision. There is no imputation among its functions, and outlier statistics belong to the data quality course. EKENE-6 is the well to be predicted, so its rows never enter a fit.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'The write-back lists the wells trained on as EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2 and on to EKENE-9. Why is EKENE-10 second?', '["EKENE-10 was the second well drilled, and the write-back lists the wells in the order they were drilled", "The shuffle at seed 5 put EKENE-10 second, and the write-back keeps the order the shuffle returned", "The engine sorts names by UTF-16 code unit, character by character, and the character 1 comes before 2", "The engine reads the number inside each name and sorts the wells by it, placing 10 after 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Names that are strings sort by UTF-16 code unit before any shuffle, so EKENE-10 comes before EKENE-2; the engine does not read the number inside a name. Natural order, EKENE-2 before EKENE-10, is the alternative it declined for a stated order that needs no parsing. The shuffled order at seed 5 begins EKENE-8, EKENE-4, EKENE-5.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'EKENE-6''s planted sonic offset, less the nine-well mean, is -5.138713 us/ft. Why can no prediction from its logs know it?', '["The ridge penalty at lambda 10 shrinks the offset toward zero before it can reach the prediction", "It is a well-level shift, and a model on the logs alone has no feature that names the well", "It is inside the training range of GR, so the range check passes it and the model ignores it", "The offset is negative, and a least squares plane can carry only a positive shift in its intercept"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each well adds its own drawn offset to every DT sample, and the intercept absorbs only the mean over the wells fitted. On the logs alone no feature names a well, so the model cannot learn a well''s offset. With the hot shale''s 8.779741 the two stated causes give 13.918453, within 1.209912 of the mean error of 12.708542.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Who chose ridge at lambda 10 on the three logs as EKENE-6''s model?', '["The course, by comparing each candidate''s mean test RMSE on the k 3, seed 5 folds and printing the scores", "The engine''s ridge function, which searches lambda over its folds and returns the lowest scoring model", "The engine''s k-fold function, which picks the features whose folds give the smallest spread of scores", "Permutation importance, which kept every feature with a positive mean drop and set lambda from the drops it read"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine does not choose lambda, the features or the model; a course or an app does that with the folds groupKFold returns and prints the scores it compared. There is no search over lambda or features among its functions. The candidates were compared on the same folds and the lowest mean, 5.826789, chose the model.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'The learning curve reads a test RMSE of 4.282693 at six wells; the k-fold at lambda 10 reads 5.826789. Which does the write-back carry as the expected error, and why?', '["The learning curve''s figure, since it uses every training well of the split and so is the most recent fit", "The lower of the two, since an expected error is best quoted at the best score any honest method gave", "The mean of the two, since each measures the same model on a different set of test wells", "The k-fold mean, which tests every well once across its folds and describes a new well like the nine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The write-back carries the k-fold mean test RMSE, 5.826789 us/ft, k 3 and seed 5: every well is tested once and the figure is the expected error for a new well like the nine. The learning curve''s test score is on one fixed set of three test wells, and one split is one draw of wells. Choosing the lower figure or averaging two schemes estimates nothing stated.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Each Newton step solves its system with `solveSPD`. When does `solveSPD` call a matrix singular?', '["When its determinant prints as zero at six decimals, the precision the course quotes every figure at", "When the scaled condition number is above 1.00e+8, the same limit least squares refuses a design at", "When a diagonal entry is at or below zero, or a scaled pivot is at or below p x machine epsilon, inclusive", "When a pivot is strictly below machine epsilon, so a pivot exactly at the bound is still accepted"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The boundary table gives the rule: a diagonal entry at or below zero, or a scaled pivot at or below p x machine epsilon, inclusive, and the rank one matrix of the refusal table shows it. The 1.00e+8 limit belongs to ols and ridge. A pivot exactly at the bound is singular, since the rule is inclusive, and the six decimal print is a quoting rule, never a test.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The engine''s own case gives a logistic probability of 0.500000. Which class does `predict` assign?', '["Class 1, since 0.5 is at the threshold and the threshold counts as pay", "Class 0: class 1 needs a probability above 0.5", "Neither, since the engine refuses a row whose probability is exactly one half", "Whichever class is the majority in training, 0 on the Ekene pay model"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The basis reads "class 1 when the probability is above 0.5, class 0 at exactly 0.5", and golden `predict-logistic-half` gives the probability 0.500000 and the class 0. The rule is strict, and a probability of one half is a result like any other, never a refusal. The engine never looks at the training labels when it assigns a class.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'In the golden `roc-ties` two rows, one positive and one negative, share the score 0.4. What does the curve do at threshold 0.400000?', '["The positive row is taken first, a vertical step to TPR 1, then the negative, a step across to FPR 0.333333 after it", "The negative row is taken first, a step across to FPR 0.333333, then the positive, a step up to TPR 1", "Both rows are skipped until the next distinct score, 0.2, since a tie at a threshold is left undecided", "Both rows are called positive together, a diagonal step from FPR 0 and TPR 0.666667 to FPR 0.333333 and TPR 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A row is called positive at a score at or above the threshold, and equal scores move together: the basis says "equal scores are one threshold: their rows move together, a diagonal step when the classes are mixed". The printed points go from (0.000000, 0.666667) to (0.333333, 1.000000). Taking either row first would favour one class, which is exactly what the rule declines.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, '`classificationReport` is given yTrue [0, 1, 1] and yPred [0, 1, 0]. How many ratios does it score with `zeroDivision`?', '["None, since zeroDivision is scored only when a denominator is 0, and here none is", "Two, one for each label, since the engine applies zeroDivision to every ratio on so few rows", "One, the recall of label 1, since one of its two rows is predicted as label 0", "One, the precision of label 0, since one of the rows predicted 0 is truly 1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary table gives this case: 0 ratios scored zeroDivision, because the rule applies only when a denominator is 0. Both labels are predicted and both appear, so every precision and recall has a nonzero denominator. A wrong prediction lowers a ratio without making it undefined; the never-predicted golden is where zeroDivision is used.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, '`groupSplit` is given 25 groups at fraction 0.28. In float 0.28 x 25 is 7 plus 8.88e-16. How many groups are held out?', '["8, the ceiling of the float product, since any excess over 7 rounds the count of test groups up", "7, since the engine rounds every product to the nearest whole number before it takes a ceiling", "7, since a product within 1.00e-9 of a whole number is taken as that number before the ceiling", "A refusal, since a fraction that leaves the product off a whole number cannot size a test set"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The test size is ceil(testFraction x count), with a product within 1.00e-9 of a whole number taken as that number. 8.88e-16 is within 1.00e-9 of 7, so 7 are held out; a plain ceiling would hold out 8. Rounding every product to the nearest whole number is a different rule, and a product far from a whole number is rounded up.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A standard scaler on GR and mudWeight is fitted on the 30 rows of EKENE-1 alone, then on the nine sonic wells. What happens each time?', '["Fitted both times, since a well-level attribute is a real measurement with a real value on every row", "Refused on EKENE-1, where every mud weight is 9.4, and fitted on the nine wells, where it varies", "Refused both times, since a well-level attribute is constant by its nature and has no spread to scale", "Fitted on EKENE-1 with a scale of 0, and fitted on the nine wells with their population SD"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A feature is refused as constant only when every training value is identical: "X.mudWeight has zero variance on the 30 training rows (every value is 9.4)". Across the nine wells the same attribute varies and is fitted. A scale of 0 would divide by zero, which is why the engine refuses instead of fitting.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'A facies label with three classes, sand, shale and lime, is passed to logistic regression as 0, 1 and 2. What does the engine do?', '["It fits one model per class against the rest, the multiclass form the engine builds on its binary fit", "It fits the three classes as ordered levels, reading 2 as more of whatever 1 stands for", "It treats every label above 0 as class 1 and fits sand against shale and lime together", "It refuses at the first label other than 0 and 1: the engine''s logistic regression is binary"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine fits binary logistic regression only, and there is no multiclass logistic. A label other than 0 or 1 is refused by name, as in "y[2] must be 0 or 1". Facies classification belongs to the electrofacies course. The engine never recodes a label on the caller''s behalf.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, '`groupSplit` is passed well names mixing strings and numbers. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming the first mismatched entry, as in `groups[1]`: all strings or all numbers", "A split after the numbers are converted to strings, so the names sort by character", "A split with the numbers sorted before the strings, then one shuffle over all of them", "A split in the order the caller passed the names, since a mixed list cannot be sorted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Strings sort by UTF-16 code unit and numbers sort ascending, and a mix of the two is refused: "groups[1] must be the same type as groups[0]: all strings or all numbers". The engine never converts a caller''s names. A shuffle always starts from the sorted list, so an unsorted order is never used.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'A caller passes seed -1 to `groupSplit`. What does the engine return?', '["A split at seed 1, since the engine takes the absolute value of a negative seed", "A split with no shuffle, the sorted order, since a negative seed turns shuffling off", "A refusal naming `seed`: seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295", "A split at a seed drawn from the clock, since a negative seed asks for a fresh one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative seed is refused by name: "seed must be a whole number from 0 to 4294967295". Every draw is mulberry32 with a stated seed, and nothing in the engine draws from the clock. A refusal changes no input on the caller''s behalf, so no split comes back at all.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Which of these figures from the course can be quoted without a seed?', '["GR''s mean permutation drop, 4.747694 us/ft", "Longley''s scaled condition number, 43275.043587", "The learning curve''s test RMSE at three wells, 4.385966", "The k-fold mean test RMSE of the chosen model, 5.826789"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Longley figure comes from a published design with no random draw. GR''s drop is measured at seed 5 with 5 repeats and moves to 4.954327 at seed 6. The learning curve holds out its test wells with a split at fraction 0.3 and seed 5, and the k-fold deals shuffled wells at k 3 and seed 5. Every seeded number is quoted with its seed.'),
  ('mlcore', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'A value above one is passed as the first probability to `logLoss`. Does the clip to [eps, 1 - eps] bring it into range?', '["Yes: it is clipped to 1 - eps and charged like any other confident probability", "Yes: it is clipped to 1 and counted among the rows clipped", "No: it is dropped from the mean and the loss is taken over the other rows", "No: it is refused first, since probabilities[0] must be a number from 0 to 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clip applies to a probability inside [0, 1] that sits closer to 0 or 1 than eps; a value outside [0, 1] is no probability, and the engine refuses it by name: "probabilities[0] must be a number from 0 to 1". A refusal returns no loss at all, so no row is dropped and nothing is counted as clipped.');
